# =============================================================================
#  Sharpline — repository-root build-context exclusions
#
#  WHY THIS FILE IS AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT
#  --------------------------------------
#  BuildKit resolves the ignore file for a build in exactly this order:
#
#      1. <dockerfile-path>.dockerignore      (per-Dockerfile; wins outright)
#      2. <context-root>/.dockerignore        (this file; the fallback)
#
#  There is no third lookup. A file at, say, `deploy/docker/.dockerignore` is
#  never consulted for a build whose context is the repository root, no matter
#  which Dockerfile under `deploy/docker/` is used. Every build in this repo
#  passes the repository root as its context:
#
#      Makefile  build-%          docker build --file deploy/docker/go.Dockerfile  $(ROOT_DIR)
#      compose.yaml       x-go-build     context: ../..   dockerfile: deploy/docker/go.Dockerfile
#      compose.tools.yaml x-tools-image  context: ../..   dockerfile: deploy/docker/tools.Dockerfile
#      compose.tools.yaml playwright     context: ../..   dockerfile: deploy/docker/playwright.Dockerfile
#      compose.tools.yaml locust         context: ../..   dockerfile: deploy/docker/locust.Dockerfile
#
#  So this file is the one that applies to all of them — with one exception:
#  the Go service build has its own sibling, `deploy/docker/go.Dockerfile.dockerignore`,
#  which takes precedence and prunes far more aggressively (it drops every
#  first-party tree the Go compiler never reads, so a frontend edit cannot
#  invalidate the Go build cache).
#
#  SCOPE OF THIS FILE — deliberately CONSERVATIVE
#  ----------------------------------------------
#  This is the fallback for builds whose Dockerfile has no sibling ignore file,
#  including ones added later by someone who never reads this comment. It
#  therefore excludes ONLY things that are never legitimately needed inside any
#  image: version-control metadata, host-produced dependency and build output,
#  secrets, caches, and OS/editor noise.
#
#  It does NOT exclude first-party source trees (web/, deploy/, docs/, test/,
#  e2e/, load/, analysis/, flink/, migrations/). Removing a source tree here
#  would silently break any future Dockerfile that legitimately COPYs it, and a
#  missing-file failure inside a build stage is far harder to diagnose than a
#  slightly larger context. Per-build pruning belongs in a per-Dockerfile
#  sibling, where its blast radius is one build.
#
#  Two entries below are load-bearing and must not be "tidied up":
#    * `migrations` is NOT excluded — the `migrate` binary embeds the goose SQL
#      via go:embed, so it must be inside the Go build context.
#    * `!.env.example` re-includes the committed template after `.env.*` removes
#      the real secrets file. Order matters; a later broad pattern would undo it.
# =============================================================================


# ── Version control and CI metadata ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Never needed in an image, and .git changes on every commit, which would
# invalidate the context digest for builds that are otherwise identical.
# Safe because go.Dockerfile builds with `-buildvcs=false`.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github


# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A file in the build context is readable by every stage and can be baked into a
# layer by an over-broad COPY. Real credentials must never get that close.
# `.env.example` is the committed, non-secret template and is deliberately kept.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
secrets/
secrets.yaml
*.dec.yaml
kubeconfig
kubeconfig.*


# ── Host-produced Node output ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLAUDE.md §7: node_modules and .next live on named Docker volumes and are
# produced inside a build stage. Anything the host produced carries the host's
# architecture (sharp, SWC, esbuild) and is a contaminant, not a shortcut.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/.turbo
web/out
.npm
.pnpm-store
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*


# ── Host-produced Go output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Builds happen in containers (prime directive), so none of this should exist on
# the host. Excluding it means a stray host `go build` cannot leak a host-arch
# binary into an image.
bin/
dist/
build/
vendor/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test
__debug_bin*
coverage.out
coverage.*
cover.html
*.prof
*.pprof


# ── Terraform state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# State files can contain credentials in plaintext. The lockfile is committed and
# is intentionally not excluded.
**/.terraform
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example
crash.log
crash.*.log
.terraformrc
terraform.rc


# ── Test and load-run artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Output of a previous run, never an input to a build.
e2e/test-results
e2e/playwright-report
e2e/blob-report
e2e/.cache
playwright/.auth
load/reports
*_stats.csv
*_stats_history.csv
*_failures.csv


# ── Python bytecode and virtualenvs (load/ and analysis/) ────────────────────
# CLAUDE.md §2 keeps Python out of the serving path entirely; its build detritus
# has no business in any image.
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.venv
venv
.ipynb_checkpoints
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
analysis/data


# ── Local runtime scratch ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
tmp/
.tmp/
.cache/


# ── Editor and OS noise ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode
.idea
*.iml
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Agent worktrees are sibling checkouts of this same repo living inside it.
# They must never enter a build context: they would bust every cache layer on
# an unrelated agent's edit, and they carry a second go.mod.
.claude/
